WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.819
A table orders ten dishes on one ticket, each with a small recipe card of its own.

2
00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:16.559
At the pass, the counter where orders come in, the head cook reads the ticket line by line and hands each dish to a helper at a free burner.

3
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:20.559
The stove has four burners, so four dishes cook at once.

4
00:00:20.559 --> 00:00:24.556
At 100 milliseconds a dish, ten dishes take three rounds.

5
00:00:24.556 --> 00:00:26.168
About 300 milliseconds.

6
00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:30.314
Kotlin has an operator for exactly this kitchen: flatMapMerge.

7
00:00:30.314 --> 00:00:33.316
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:33.316 --> 00:00:38.212
If I give it four burners, doesn't everything inside it run four at a time?

9
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.999
A flow is a recipe card for values, so the ticket is a flow of dish names.

10
00:00:43.999 --> 00:00:50.041
stove.cook is a suspend call: it pauses for 100 milliseconds without holding a thread.

11
00:00:50.041 --> 00:00:55.239
The helper's lambda cooks the dish, then returns a flow of that one plate.

12
00:00:55.239 --> 00:00:56.996
Ten dishes, four burners.

13
00:00:56.996 --> 00:00:57.628
How long?

14
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.465
1,000 milliseconds.

15
00:01:01.465 --> 00:01:06.592
One dish on the stove at a time, more than three times as long as we planned.

16
00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:07.406
Picture it.

17
00:01:07.406 --> 00:01:11.504
Four helpers at four warm burners, each holding an empty pan.

18
00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:13.386
The calmest kitchen in town.

19
00:01:13.386 --> 00:01:15.939
So the cap of four wasn't the problem.

20
00:01:15.939 --> 00:01:18.156
Something before the burners was.

21
00:01:18.633 --> 00:01:19.900
Watch the head cook.

22
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:24.459
She does whatever the lambda says before she hands anything to a burner.

23
00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:26.106
This lambda says: cook it.

24
00:01:26.106 --> 00:01:28.132
So she cooks every dish herself.

25
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:29.668
Let's map it.

26
00:01:29.668 --> 00:01:31.604
The ticket is the outer flow.

27
00:01:31.604 --> 00:01:36.278
The flow the lambda returns is the dish's recipe card: the inner flow.

28
00:01:36.278 --> 00:01:39.683
The burners are lanes, and concurrency counts them.

29
00:01:39.683 --> 00:01:43.489
The head cook is the one coroutine that runs your lambda.

30
00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:45.844
Built by hand, it's short.

31
00:01:45.844 --> 00:01:47.907
One coroutine collects the ticket.

32
00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.819
For each dish, it calls your lambda, right here.

33
00:01:50.819 --> 00:01:54.884
Then it takes a permit from a semaphore, a counter of free burners.

34
00:01:54.884 --> 00:01:58.039
And it launches a lane that collects the inner flow.

35
00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:03.455
So the lambda runs in the one reading coroutine, before a burner is even asked for.

36
00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:05.827
Only the flow it returns gets a lane.

37
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.461
So the slow call goes inside the returned flow.

38
00:02:09.461 --> 00:02:14.541
Now the head cook only writes the recipe card, and the helpers do the cooking.

39
00:02:14.541 --> 00:02:17.407
Reactor's flatMap in Java has the same trap.

40
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.842
Let's shrink it to five dishes on two burners.

41
00:02:21.842 --> 00:02:30.195
Soup takes 300 milliseconds, salad 100, roast 200, bread 50 and pie 150, on the lesson's fake clock.

42
00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:34.304
With the fix, the lambda runs for soup, salad and roast at 0.

43
00:02:34.304 --> 00:02:36.488
So when does roast start cooking?

44
00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:41.767
At 100, when the salad frees a burner.

45
00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:45.818
The order is ready at 450, with two on the stove at most.

46
00:02:45.818 --> 00:02:50.508
And plates leave as they're ready: salad, soup, roast, bread, pie.

47
00:02:50.508 --> 00:02:52.213
That's completion order.

48
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.834
Now put the cooking back in the lambda.

49
00:02:54.834 --> 00:02:59.187
800 milliseconds: the sum of all five, what a plain map would take.

50
00:02:59.187 --> 00:03:00.747
The burners did nothing.

51
00:03:00.833 --> 00:03:03.927
The rule: the lambda only builds the inner flow.

52
00:03:03.927 --> 00:03:05.732
Put the slow work inside it.

53
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:10.396
flatMapMerge is one of three ways to run inner flows.

54
00:03:10.396 --> 00:03:22.008
To compare them, a table sends three orders, 100 milliseconds apart, and each opens a recipe in two parts: part a after 50 milliseconds, part b 150 later.

55
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.154
flatMapConcat cooks one order at a time.

56
00:03:25.154 --> 00:03:27.079
So when does order 2 come in?

57
00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:31.422
Most people say 100.

58
00:03:31.422 --> 00:03:34.757
It's 300, and the whole run takes 800.

59
00:03:34.833 --> 00:03:41.590
flatMapConcat has no second coroutine and no shelf: each recipe runs inside the outer flow's emit.

60
00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:48.002
Like the cook from the first lesson, the waiter stands at the pass until both parts are done.

61
00:03:48.002 --> 00:03:51.173
Only then does his 100 millisecond wait begin.

62
00:03:51.267 --> 00:03:53.345
Here the kitchen picture breaks.

63
00:03:53.345 --> 00:03:56.658
A real waiter would walk back while the dish cooks.

64
00:03:56.658 --> 00:04:00.100
This one can't: even his waiting happens inside emit.

65
00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:04.712
Put buffer in front, and he leaves orders on a shelf: 600 milliseconds.

66
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.395
flatMapMerge gives each order its own burner.

67
00:04:08.395 --> 00:04:11.031
Orders come in at 0, 100 and 200.

68
00:04:11.031 --> 00:04:15.345
Parts come out as they're ready, so 1b comes after 2a.

69
00:04:15.345 --> 00:04:16.304
Done at 400.

70
00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:19.130
flatMapLatest keeps only the newest.

71
00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:25.727
Each new order cancels the recipe in flight, waits for it to stop, and starts the next.

72
00:04:25.727 --> 00:04:27.698
So 1b and 2b never arrive.

73
00:04:27.698 --> 00:04:28.608
Done at 400.

74
00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:34.224
The table changed its mind twice, and the kitchen binned two half made dishes without a word.

75
00:04:34.224 --> 00:04:36.006
Every waiter knows that table.

76
00:04:36.006 --> 00:04:41.648
It's the shape you want for a search box, where each new letter makes the old search worthless.

77
00:04:42.433 --> 00:04:44.868
Three operators, three promises.

78
00:04:44.868 --> 00:04:48.673
flatMapConcat: one at a time, and the outer waits.

79
00:04:48.673 --> 00:04:52.325
flatMapMerge: side by side, in completion order.

80
00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:54.684
flatMapLatest: only the newest.

81
00:04:54.767 --> 00:04:55.634
Three rules.

82
00:04:55.634 --> 00:05:01.560
Set the burners from what the stove can take: the default is 16, Reactor's is 256.

83
00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:06.402
Keep a cancelled recipe's cleanup short: the next one waits for it.

84
00:05:06.402 --> 00:05:10.665
And if one after another is fine, a plain map reads better.

85
00:05:11.233 --> 00:05:15.096
One limit: a burnt dish in any lane cancels all the others.

86
00:05:15.096 --> 00:05:16.601
That's the next lesson.

87
00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:21.436
So, does everything inside flatMapMerge run four at a time?

88
00:05:21.436 --> 00:05:21.647
No.

89
00:05:21.647 --> 00:05:28.377
The head cook runs your lambda one dish at a time, and only the recipe it returns gets a burner.

90
00:05:28.377 --> 00:05:31.812
Cook there, and ten dishes take 300 milliseconds.

91
00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:32.796
Quick check.

92
00:05:32.796 --> 00:05:37.727
The outer emits a value, then waits 100 milliseconds, three times.

93
00:05:37.727 --> 00:05:40.491
Each inner gives one value after 200.

94
00:05:40.491 --> 00:05:42.956
How long does flatMapConcat take?

95
00:05:45.533 --> 00:05:52.451
900 milliseconds: three times 200 plus 100, because the outer waits inside emit.

96
00:05:52.451 --> 00:05:54.699
With buffer in front, 600.
